{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/474902-scion-ragnarok-idea","id":474902,"title":"Scion: Ragnarok Idea","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-09T14:33:27-07:00","id":474902,"title":"Scion: Ragnarok Idea","content":"Hey all.<br /> <br /> So, I'm currently Storytelling a <i>Scion: Ragnarok</i> campaign and we've just completed <i>The Coming of Winter</i>, with Fimbulvetr in full effect. I didn't want to just jump ahead one year without exploring some story between and one of the Scions is a Son of Heimdall and a Delta Force Operator, which gave me an idea. In the aftermath of the long winter, since the line between mortal and divine is blurry, some form of joint special operations taskforce is created between Aesir Scions and what remains of mortal military who haven't completely lost themselves to Titanic influence. This would make the Band the 'elite' strike team of said force.<br /> <br /> Thoughts on if this sounds like a feasible idea? Comments? Suggestions?<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5619-starkjunior","name":"StarkJunior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-10T12:16:29-07:00","id":476190,"title":"","content":"Feasible or not - if you think it`s a fun idea, and the players agree, who`s to say you`re wrong? It`s your game. Are you going for a grim saga-like story there, or something more optimistic?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5530-xanthias","name":"Xanthias","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-10T12:25:03-07:00","id":476197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xanthias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/474902-scion-ragnarok-idea?p=476190#post476190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Feasible or not - if you think it`s a fun idea, and the players agree, who`s to say you`re wrong? It`s your game. Are you going for a grim saga-like story there, or something more optimistic?</div> </div> </div> </div>Optimistic, I think. Considering the Norse sagas have considerable tales of heroism and such, something like this would be fitting for the themes that have developed in the campaign. Namely, the idea that even if their parents are doomed to Fate, they might not be and could do something about it.<br /> <br /> Regardless of feelings on the quality of the films, the idea is a little similiar to NEST from the <i>Transformers </i>series, except replace the Autobots with the Aesir and Deceptions with the jotuns and their allies. I even started bouncing some ideas around for unit names, like MJOLNIR or Tyr's Hand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5619-starkjunior","name":"StarkJunior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-11T10:36:37-07:00","id":477077,"title":"","content":"Are they giving the orders? Most special ops teams, fictional or not, get their missions from someone else. Usually someone with the authority to tell them what to do... So, who is that? If it's a mortal, WTF? We're GODS niggling petty mortals... If its another divine power it's essentially a skin on the &quot;do what your parents/insert authority figure say&quot;. Not a bad one as it really nails down character concepts as &quot;what part do you play on this team&quot;? If they are semi-independent/independent (meaning they are essentially doing this for their &quot;pet&quot; mortals b/c they don't want to see anymore of them die than already have) then it's not really a military-style game, it's a band of superheroes that play well with the military when they feel like it.<br /> <br /> If it's a military spec ops kinda game, some one character is going to be the one in charge of the team. That's something the players have to buy into. And if it's mixed military/non-military personnel, or even people attached to another chain of command, it can undermine the &quot;conceit&quot; if people just ignore the Captain when he gives the relevant orders.<br /> <br /> It can be fun, but you have to really think about how the game and the chosen mode of operations will intersect, or more appropriately, when they don't, so you are ready up front to deal with them.<br /> <br /> We did a short game where the characters were a spec ops unit in Iraq during the occupation put together by Tyr (fronting an inclusive group of interested deities) to get all the important crap that had been stolen from the Baghdad museum during the chaos. We had the Lt. in charge (son of Tyr). He had an entire unit of Jotunblutted grunts. There was an intelligence office (Tezcatlipoca) and a sniper (Artemis). There was a doctor on board as well. (Tuatha and I can't remember the parent). And an archaeolologist (daughter of Thoth). The archaeologist worked as she was part of an international effort under the aegis of the normal mortal allies to figure out what had happened to the museum collection. So she fit right in and was very happy to have capable killing machines around her to protect her. The doctor was a disaster. She wasn't military. She didn't have any connection to the main plot of the game. She didn't obey orders. She would work directly against the other PCs when they wanted to do things like kill the enemy. That player's first idea, the helicopter pilot, was a better fit for the game. We agreed not to do it b/c she would have a hard time being involved in adventures if her job was to get people to the interesting place, drop them off, stand by until they needed evac, then do that. So, not going with them into the mysterious ziggurat that had come up out of the sand and all. Which wouldn't have been terribly fun to play, unless you had a lot of reading you needed to get done while everyone else was playing. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-11T20:05:02-07:00","id":477457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/474902-scion-ragnarok-idea?p=477077#post477077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are they giving the orders? Most special ops teams, fictional or not, get their missions from someone else. Usually someone with the authority to tell them what to do... So, who is that? If it's a mortal, WTF? We're GODS niggling petty mortals... If its another divine power it's essentially a skin on the &quot;do what your parents/insert authority figure say&quot;. Not a bad one as it really nails down character concepts as &quot;what part do you play on this team&quot;? If they are semi-independent/independent (meaning they are essentially doing this for their &quot;pet&quot; mortals b/c they don't want to see anymore of them die than already have) then it's not really a military-style game, it's a band of superheroes that play well with the military when they feel like it.<br /> <br /> If it's a military spec ops kinda game, some one character is going to be the one in charge of the team. That's something the players have to buy into. And if it's mixed military/non-military personnel, or even people attached to another chain of command, it can undermine the &quot;conceit&quot; if people just ignore the Captain when he gives the relevant orders.<br /> <br /> It can be fun, but you have to really think about how the game and the chosen mode of operations will intersect, or more appropriately, when they don't, so you are ready up front to deal with them.<br /> <br /> We did a short game where the characters were a spec ops unit in Iraq during the occupation put together by Tyr (fronting an inclusive group of interested deities) to get all the important crap that had been stolen from the Baghdad museum during the chaos. We had the Lt. in charge (son of Tyr). He had an entire unit of Jotunblutted grunts. There was an intelligence office (Tezcatlipoca) and a sniper (Artemis). There was a doctor on board as well. (Tuatha and I can't remember the parent). And an archaeolologist (daughter of Thoth). The archaeologist worked as she was part of an international effort under the aegis of the normal mortal allies to figure out what had happened to the museum collection. So she fit right in and was very happy to have capable killing machines around her to protect her. The doctor was a disaster. She wasn't military. She didn't have any connection to the main plot of the game. She didn't obey orders. She would work directly against the other PCs when they wanted to do things like kill the enemy. That player's first idea, the helicopter pilot, was a better fit for the game. We agreed not to do it b/c she would have a hard time being involved in adventures if her job was to get people to the interesting place, drop them off, stand by until they needed evac, then do that. So, not going with them into the mysterious ziggurat that had come up out of the sand and all. Which wouldn't have been terribly fun to play, unless you had a lot of reading you needed to get done while everyone else was playing.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it'll be too much of a problem. The Son of Heimdall is a Delta Force Operator, the Son of Baldur is a cop, the Daughter of Thor is a stunt woman, and the Daughter of Loki is a survivor and is very set on proving she's not her father. The idea was basically that since nobody is hiding the existence of the gods, it would eventually come to the realization that something needs to be done. The Son of Heimdall has already been in command during battle and tactical situations, and likely an older Scion of Tyr or some similiar god will be giving orders, but I would have it that they are also in the military.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5619-starkjunior","name":"StarkJunior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-12T08:48:20-07:00","id":477971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StarkJunior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/474902-scion-ragnarok-idea?p=477457#post477457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think it'll be too much of a problem. The Son of Heimdall is a Delta Force Operator, the Son of Baldur is a cop, the Daughter of Thor is a stunt woman, and the Daughter of Loki is a survivor and is very set on proving she's not her father. The idea was basically that since nobody is hiding the existence of the gods, it would eventually come to the realization that something needs to be done. The Son of Heimdall has already been in command during battle and tactical situations, and likely an older Scion of Tyr or some similiar god will be giving orders, but I would have it that they are also in the military.</div> </div> </div> </div>Make 'em all officers. Then you don't have issues with people issuing them commands that are effectively irrelevant (except maybe somebody who is clever enough to have hidden some of that juicy Dark Virtue and is the martinet type, then it's a plot point.). Have them in off in their own special ops group out of the normal Chain of Command (CoC), responding directly to their their group leader and him to someone who plays the &quot;Mr. Jones&quot;/mission giver type and THAT guy directly to whoever is complete command. So, like all Lt., Captains, Majors (or equivalent) with the team/band leader as a Colonel or light Colonel. Report to General so and so Scion who reports to the Joint Chiefs or the President or whatever. Alternatively, like the Avengers report to Sam Jackson, I mean Nick Fury who is responsible to the Directors of SHIELD. It will give the whole military feel a little verisimilitude. <br /> <br /> Still and all, if they ultimate leadership is mere humans, why are they listening to them? Unless that's a plot point as well. After all, they DO know more, are better positioned, etc. than any mortal will ever be for the eschatalogical disaster looming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}